WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.287
Hi, I'm Galette!

00:00:02.287 --> 00:00:04.613
Translation needs a reader and a writer.

00:00:04.613 --> 00:00:06.608
Here's how one Transformer is both.

00:00:08.892 --> 00:00:11.361
An encoder reads the whole source at once.

00:00:11.361 --> 00:00:15.065
A decoder writes the target one token at a time, looking back

00:00:15.065 --> 00:00:16.609
at the source through cross-attention.

00:00:19.425 --> 00:00:23.020
The encoder reads the source both ways and hands over one vector

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:24.218
per word: the memory.

00:00:24.638 --> 00:00:28.765
The decoder reads the target so far, masked so no word sees the future.

00:00:29.185 --> 00:00:33.347
Cross-attention: queries from the decoder, keys and values from the encoder.

00:00:33.347 --> 00:00:36.374
The newest word asks which source word matters.

00:00:36.794 --> 00:00:40.627
An MLP and a softmax pick the next word, which joins the target.

00:00:42.125 --> 00:00:44.976
In the encoder, every source word sees every other.

00:00:45.396 --> 00:00:48.603
In the decoder, each target word sees only its past.

00:00:48.603 --> 00:00:53.093
Training feeds it the true target, shifted right, and predicts every word at once.

00:00:53.513 --> 00:00:57.504
Across the bridge, no mask: every target word may read the whole source.

00:00:59.108 --> 00:01:01.624
To pretrain the pair, T5 corrupts text.

00:01:01.624 --> 00:01:04.859
Pick fifteen percent of the tokens, in short spans.

00:01:05.279 --> 00:01:07.548
Replace each span with a single placeholder.

00:01:08.433 --> 00:01:10.565
The encoder reads the sentence with holes.

00:01:11.586 --> 00:01:13.995
The decoder writes only what's missing.

00:01:13.995 --> 00:01:16.404
BART instead rebuilds the whole original.

00:01:17.633 --> 00:01:21.928
Each token, source or target, passes through only half the weights: about

00:01:21.928 --> 00:01:24.433
twenty-two million multiply-adds in the base model.

00:01:26.025 --> 00:01:30.010
But it needs a separate source, and every new chat turn means encoding again.

00:01:30.698 --> 00:01:34.541
A decoder-only model trains on any text with one loss, takes its

00:01:34.541 --> 00:01:36.783
task from the prompt, and simply appends.

00:01:38.392 --> 00:01:39.963
Two stacks, joined by cross-attention.

00:01:39.963 --> 00:01:41.849
One reads the source both ways.

00:01:41.849 --> 00:01:44.049
The other writes, looking back at it.

00:01:46.692 --> 00:01:48.398
Read it all, write it out, look back.

00:01:51.292 --> 00:01:53.384
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:53.384 --> 00:01:53.982
I'm Galette.

00:01:53.982 --> 00:01:54.281
Bye!
